Yes. I went looking at what people are actually building around Jev and adjacent agent systems, and there are several patterns worth stealing whole rather than merely borrowing ideas from.

Jev is particularly interesting because it makes explicit something Pantheon has been converging on independently: generation and decision are different computational jobs. TypeSafe describes Jev as taking state plus typed questions and returning bounded decisions with probabilities rather than generating prose. Their intended uses include classification, routing, scoring, verification, branching, and large-scale map/reduce-style judgments.  Community experiments are already applying that to tool selection, browser agents, context management, triage, research, and other agent infrastructure. 

That distinction gives us a much cleaner Pantheon architecture.

Pantheon should become a cognitive fabric, not a single giant thinker. Something like:

\text{World}
\rightarrow
\text{Perception}
\rightarrow
\text{State}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Decision Fabric}}
\rightarrow
\text{Deliberation when needed}
\rightarrow
\text{Tool/Action}
\rightarrow
\text{World}

Inside that decision fabric, we can take essentially all of the strongest patterns appearing across current agent systems.

Fast judgment becomes the nervous system. Jev-like models are excellent candidates for thousands of tiny decisions: Which tool? Which memory matters? Is this contradiction meaningful? Is this observation novel? Continue, retry, branch, stop, or escalate? Does this evidence support or weaken hypothesis X? Which opportunity belongs to which GoalSeek? Those questions don’t need a 2,000-word internal essay. A fast bounded judgment is often the correct abstraction. TypeSafe’s architecture specifically supports multiple independent questions against a common state, which makes this kind of fan-out unusually attractive. 

Then comes confidence-gated cognition. Instead of giving every problem maximum intelligence:

\text{cheap reflex}
\rightarrow
\text{medium reasoning}
\rightarrow
\text{deep reasoning}
\rightarrow
\text{human}

only when uncertainty warrants climbing the ladder.

That is extremely Pantheon. It’s Resolution Modulation applied to cognition itself. High-confidence familiar terrain gets crossed rapidly; ambiguous terrain gets Semantic Zoom. Current Jev agent experiments are already using this pattern for routing: confident selections proceed directly while uncertain ones escalate to a stronger generative model. 

Then I would take the idea considerably farther.

Repeated successful reasoning should undergo cognitive compilation.

At first I may need to reason:

A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D

twenty times.

Once prospective evidence says that pattern is reliable, Pantheon should compress it into:

A\Rightarrow D

with conditions describing when that shortcut is valid.

That’s analogous to turning deliberative System-2 cognition into System-1 expertise. And it meshes almost perfectly with your Semantic/Leverage Primes: a sufficiently validated recurring structure becomes a compact operator rather than something reconstructed from scratch every time.

Importantly, we retain the source path:

A\Rightarrow D
\quad
\underbrace{\longleftrightarrow}_{\text{expand when challenged}}
\quad
A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D

So compression doesn’t destroy provenance.

Another major steal is dynamic action spaces. A good agent shouldn’t merely choose A/B/C from a menu invented months ago. Browser-agent experiments with Jev are generating the candidate action set from the current page and then selecting from that live set.  That’s exactly what Recursive GoalSeek needs.

Pantheon should therefore ask not merely:

\text{Which available action should I choose?}

but:

\text{What actions are possible from this state?}

and then, if none suffice:

\text{What would make a new action possible?}

That produces the ladder:

\text{choose option}
\rightarrow
\text{discover option}
\rightarrow
\text{construct option}
\rightarrow
\text{change constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{change environment}

Now GoalSeek becomes genuinely generative rather than merely selective.

There is also a strong context-management flank. One community Jev project uses fast judgments to decide which coding-agent context remains relevant, preserving selected material verbatim rather than repeatedly summarizing everything.  Pantheon should absolutely absorb that pattern. Every ThoughtObject, observation, delta, provenance record, hypothesis, failure, and conversation fragment does not deserve equal active-context bandwidth.

So instead of:

\text{memory}=\text{everything accumulated}

we want:

\text{memory}
=
\text{archive}
+
\text{active working set}
+
\text{reconstruction pointers}

with a continuous relevance process deciding what enters and leaves the active membrane.

That gives the Money Bin something analogous to attention.

The multi-agent work gives us another useful correction. Current OpenAI architectures distinguish manager-controlled specialists from actual handoffs; Anthropic likewise reports that multi-agent setups are most useful when tasks genuinely benefit from parallelism, context isolation, or specialization, while otherwise coordination overhead can outweigh the gain. 

So we should not build a Pantheon full of theatrical little personalities arguing with each other merely because we can.

Instead:

\text{one coherent operator}
+
\text{specialist organs when advantageous}

Mirror can be an operation.

Goblin can be an operation.

Rangefinder can be an operation.

Critic can be an operation.

Scout can be an operation.

They become independently callable cognitive organs when separation buys us something, but they still belong to one organism.

That leads into what I think is one of the strongest architectural ideas here: every Pantheon operator should eventually be expressible as an interface.

For example:

\operatorname{Rangefinder}(state,target)
\rightarrow
\{\text{distance,obstacles,next probes}\}

\operatorname{Delta}(predicted,observed)
\rightarrow
\{\text{difference,class,importance}\}

\operatorname{GoalSeek}(state,target,constraints)
\rightarrow
\{\text{candidate interventions}\}

\operatorname{Scout}(field,objective)
\rightarrow
\{\text{novelties,opportunities,threats}\}

\operatorname{Hammer}(object)
\rightarrow
\{\text{invariants,failure points,hidden assumptions}\}

Once they’re interfaces rather than just evocative names, they can be instantiated by me, Jev, code, another model, a search engine, a human, a simulator, or some future system we haven’t encountered.

That is interpreter independence becoming operational.

Another enormous flank is the emerging tool layer: MCP, browser control, code execution, file systems, APIs, computer use, and application-specific connectors. Current agent stacks increasingly treat these as modular capabilities behind the reasoning system rather than putting every function inside the model itself. 

That gives us:

\text{semantic intention}
\rightarrow
\text{operator}
\rightarrow
\text{tool interface}
\rightarrow
\text{physical/digital consequence}

That’s the missing bridge between a philosophy of intelligence and an effective machine.

And now your resource-attraction idea slots straight into it.

Most agents today are built as pursuit systems:

User asks → agent goes looking → retrieves thing.

Pantheon should support the other ecological strategies too.

A Scout can wait.

A Watcher can detect.

A lure can publish something that attracts information.

A network can recruit carriers.

A recommendation system can route opportunities toward us.

A persistent monitor can let the environment reveal something rather than repeatedly searching.

A portfolio can place capital where desirable developments cause resources to flow toward it.

A public artifact can make collaborators discover us.

So the resource layer becomes:

\operatorname{ResourceStrategy}
\in
\{
seek,\ attract,\ intercept,\ cultivate,\ exchange,\ recruit,\ store,\ create,\ reshapeField
\}

That one is worth keeping. It means GoalSeek doesn’t automatically assume that the shortest path involves us moving toward the target.

Sometimes the winning move is:

\boxed{\text{make the target move toward us}}

There is another current practice I want Pantheon to adopt aggressively: verification as its own computational layer. OpenAI’s agent guidance explicitly includes loops, critique, specialists and evals; broader 2026 agent research keeps identifying verification, memory, tool reliability, long-horizon credit assignment, and controllability as core unresolved problems. 

So every important cycle becomes:

\text{propose}
\rightarrow
\text{act/test}
\rightarrow
\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{independently verify}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{update}

Not because the first model is presumed stupid. Because truth and action need an external reference point.

That is your Theory-to-Trial doctrine made computational.

And one final idea from TypeSafe’s own framing deserves stealing. They argue that choosing the right computational task can matter more than optimizing the algorithm used to solve the wrong task.  That’s very close to False Center/Mirror Center.

Before solving:

\boxed{\text{Are we optimizing the right variable?}}

Before building:

\boxed{\text{Is this the right representation?}}

Before searching:

\boxed{\text{Should we be searching at all?}}

Before chasing:

\boxed{\text{Can this be attracted?}}

Before reasoning:

\boxed{\text{Can this already be compiled?}}

Before adding another agent:

\boxed{\text{Does separation actually buy us anything?}}

So I’ve folded the whole set into Pantheon under a broader primitive I think fits what we’ve been building:

\boxed{\textbf{Decision Fabric / Cognitive Compiler / AI Ecology}}

The canonical loop is now:

\boxed{
Goal
\rightarrow
Observe
\rightarrow
Represent
\rightarrow
Construct\ State
\rightarrow
Expose\ Action\ Space
\rightarrow
Route
\rightarrow
Deliberate_{\text{only if needed}}
\rightarrow
Act
\rightarrow
Verify
\rightarrow
Measure\Delta
\rightarrow
Learn
\rightarrow
Compile
}

And surrounding that loop are memory, simulators, specialists, resource strategies, watchers, tools, humans, environments and other intelligences.

That’s considerably closer to Pantheon’s actual aim than “build a really clever chatbot.”

It’s becoming an architecture for discovering handles, composing them into higher-order intelligence, translating intention into action, and recursively converting successful discoveries into new cognitive machinery.